            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-EzUAdDYb5osCmVxzSDFlgC4EgHe\">Overall, teachers in the USA spent the most time teaching across all educational levels compared to the other three nations. Additionally, in Spain, upper secondary teachers had notably higher teaching hours than their primary and lower secondary counterparts, while the differences between levels in other countries were relatively modest.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13842,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 614,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-E3ZGdCcQPoFgMvxSPDRle4pMgic\">Looking at the USA and Spain, American teachers' annual teaching hours varied significantly, with a gap of around 270 hours between the lowest at primary level (830 hours) and the highest at upper secondary level (1100 hours), while lower secondary educators taught for 1080 hours. In Spain, while primary and lower secondary teachers spent about 600 hours teaching, their upper secondary colleagues worked substantially more, at nearly 980 hours per year.</div>\n</div>",
